;; spec:   stdlib §2.3 (Stack), §4.4 row pop, §2.5 (the bulk builder), §8.1 item 5 (count cases), tranche 2 plan Y5
;; expect: accept
;; result: 639
;; audit:  clean
;; allocs: <= 108
;; roots:  ../../lib support
;; covers: pop
;; Until the prelude has vec-pop (task P1) the pop of a Vec rebuilds the first n-1 elements with the bulk builder, in one
;; pass: 2 objects for every 32 elements and a few constants, where a conj loop spends 2.09 an element. The vector of 640
;; (built by the same builder from a range) costs 54 objects, measured alone; the pop adds 54 more, 108 in all. A rebuild
;; by conj would add about 1340. The bound is the count as measured under both tools, so it fails if the pop falls back to a
;; conj loop; it passes, loosely, the day vec-pop makes the pop cheaper, and case 2566 is the one that waits for P1.
(ns main (:use fib.core fib.seq fib.coll))

(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((v (vec (range 640))))
    (count (pop v))))
